# osv.dadl -- OSV.dev vulnerability database REST API for ToolMesh
# OSV.dev is Google's open, distributed vulnerability database for open source.
# It aggregates advisories in the OSV schema (https://ossf.github.io/osv-schema/)
# from many "home databases" (GitHub Security Advisories, PyPA, Go, RustSec,
# OSS-Fuzz, Debian, Ubuntu, Alpine, AlmaLinux, Rocky Linux, SUSE, Chainguard,
# NVD-derived CVE records, ...) and answers one question precisely: which known
# vulnerabilities affect this package at this version (or this git commit)?
#
# Domain Notes for LLM consumers:
# - PUBLIC, KEY-FREE API. No account, API key or token. The auth block below is
#   the ToolMesh placeholder for APIs without authentication (credential "none").
#   No rate limit is documented ("currently there are no limits on the API").
# - SIX ENDPOINTS, all covered: POST /v1/query, POST /v1/querybatch,
#   GET /v1/vulns/{id}, and three EXPERIMENTAL ones under /v1experimental/
#   (determineversion, importfindings/{source}, ubuntu/binary-to-source).
#   Queries are POSTs with a JSON body but have no side effects: every tool is
#   access: read, and the POST tools set retry_unsafe: true because repeating a
#   query is harmless (without it ToolMesh would never retry a POST).
# - IDENTIFIERS: record ids are "<DB>-<ENTRYID>", case-sensitive (GHSA-...,
#   PYSEC-..., GO-..., RUSTSEC-..., OSV-... for OSS-Fuzz, CVE-... for records
#   converted from NVD with GIT commit ranges, DEBIAN-CVE-/DSA-/DLA-,
#   UBUNTU-CVE-/USN-, MAL-... for malicious packages, ...). A lowercase id
#   ("ghsa-...") returns HTTP 404.
# - DUPLICATES ARE NORMAL. One vulnerability usually appears under several ids:
#   GHSA-x lists PYSEC-y and CVE-z in "aliases" (= same vulnerability), and
#   distro records point to the CVE via "upstream" (UBUNTU-CVE-2024-1 ->
#   CVE-2024-1). Count distinct vulnerabilities by merging alias-linked ids.
#   Distro advisories (DSA, DLA, USN) bundle several CVEs, so do not merge them
#   via "upstream" or one advisory swallows unrelated findings.
# - ECOSYSTEM NAMES ARE CASE-SENSITIVE and validated: "PyPI", "npm", "Maven",
#   "Go", "crates.io", "RubyGems", "NuGet", "Packagist", "Pub", "Hex", "Hackage",
#   "CRAN", "SwiftURL", "GitHub Actions", "Linux", "OSS-Fuzz", "GIT", ... A wrong
#   spelling ("pypi") is HTTP 400 "invalid ecosystem", not an empty result.
#   Distro ecosystems take a release suffix: "Debian:12", "Ubuntu:24.04:LTS"
#   (or "Ubuntu:24.04"), "Alpine:v3.20", "AlmaLinux:9", "Rocky Linux:9".
#   Without the suffix ("Debian") OSV matches EVERY release of that distro and
#   the result mixes them (verified: openssl in "Debian" = 102 records,
#   in "Debian:12" = 61). Full list:
#   https://osv-vulnerabilities.storage.googleapis.com/ecosystems.txt
# - PACKAGE NAMES are matched exactly, except PyPI (PEP 503 normalization:
#   "Jinja2" == "jinja2"), NuGet (case-insensitive) and crates.io
#   (case-insensitive, "-" == "_"). Maven names are "groupId:artifactId";
#   the Go standard library is name "stdlib" in ecosystem "Go"; the Linux kernel
#   is name "Kernel" in ecosystem "Linux". Distro queries use the SOURCE package
#   name (Debian/Ubuntu "openssl", not "libssl3"); map Ubuntu binary names with
#   map_ubuntu_binary_packages first.
# - VERSIONS must be the ecosystem's exact version string. Distro packages need
#   the full package version incl. epoch and revision ("1:2.3.4-1+deb12u1",
#   "3.3.1-r0" on Alpine). Omitting the version returns every vulnerability
#   ever recorded for the package (all versions).
# - PURL: a package may be given as {"purl": "pkg:pypi/jinja2@3.1.4"} instead of
#   {name, ecosystem}. Put the version EITHER in the purl OR in "version",
#   never both (HTTP 400 "version specified in params and PURL query").
# - COMMIT queries take a full git commit hash and match GIT ranges (mostly
#   C/C++ projects from OSS-Fuzz and NVD-converted CVE records). "package" is
#   optional then; do not combine "commit" with "version".
# - AN EMPTY RESULT IS NOT PROOF OF "CLEAN". Coverage depends on the upstream
#   feeds OSV imports: ecosystems or distros without a feed, private or vendored
#   code, packages renamed upstream, version strings in a different format than
#   the advisory and advisories not yet imported all come back empty. The API
#   answers a version that no record covers with {} (no "vulns" key); the
#   tools here normalize that to vulns: [].
# - SEVERITY: "severity" holds CVSS VECTOR STRINGS (CVSS_V2/V3/V4, e.g.
#   "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/..."), NOT numeric base scores; computing the score
#   needs a CVSS calculator. Type "Ubuntu" carries a priority word (negligible,
#   low, medium, high, critical). Labels live elsewhere: GHSA records in
#   database_specific.severity (LOW, MODERATE, HIGH, CRITICAL), OSS-Fuzz in
#   affected[].ecosystem_specific.severity, Debian in
#   affected[].ecosystem_specific.urgency. Many records (older PYSEC, Go)
#   have no severity at all. The query tools surface these as severity_label.
# - AFFECTED RANGES: affected[] lists every package (and, for distros, every
#   release) the record applies to, not only the queried one. ranges[].events
#   are {introduced}, {fixed}, {last_affected} or {limit}; ECOSYSTEM/SEMVER
#   ranges use version strings, GIT ranges commit hashes. introduced "0" means
#   "since the first version". No "fixed" event = no fix released (yet). One
#   range can hold several branches (Go stdlib: introduced 0 / fixed 1.21.8,
#   introduced 1.22.0-0 / fixed 1.22.1): pick the fixed version on the
#   installed version's branch, not the lowest one.
# - WITHDRAWN: a record with a "withdrawn" timestamp is retracted; ignore it.
# - PAGINATION uses a body token, not query params: a response may carry
#   next_page_token, and the same query is repeated with page_token set until
#   no token comes back. It happens above ~1,000 vulnerabilities per query
#   (querybatch: ~1,000 per query or ~3,000 in total) OR when the server hits
#   its 20-second time limit. In the latter case the response can contain ONLY
#   a token and zero vulns. ToolMesh's automatic pagination cannot follow body
#   tokens on object responses, so every tool sets pagination: none and
#   exposes page_token / next_page_token instead.
# - LINUX KERNEL (verified 2026-11-24): {"name": "Kernel", "ecosystem":
#   "Linux"} with version "6.6.1" paged through four 20-second rounds of
#   0 vulns + token and ended after ~87 s with NO result, although the
#   records exist: they are CVE-* records with ECOSYSTEM ranges such as
#   introduced 5.11.0 / fixed 5.15.23 (2,700+ of them, ~13 MB unfiltered).
#   Distro kernel packages (Debian "linux" at 6.1.112-1: 2,920 records,
#   12.9 MB in the first page) exceed ToolMesh's response limit (below).
#   Kernel checks therefore do not work reliably through this backend; use
#   osv-scanner or the bulk export instead.
# - RESPONSE SIZE: full records are large. Ubuntu records repeat their
#   affected entry for every Ubuntu release and Pro variant (openssl on
#   Ubuntu:24.04:LTS = 67 records, 1.75 MB). query_vulnerabilities therefore
#   projects each record (drops details, references, credits, the enumerated
#   affected[].versions list and the ecosystem_specific/database_specific
#   blobs); get_vulnerability returns the complete record. The API caps
#   responses at 32 MiB on HTTP/1.1, but ToolMesh reads at most 10 MiB of a
#   response BEFORE the projection runs: a larger body is cut off, the jq
#   projection fails on the truncated JSON and the call returns the raw text
#   as a STRING instead of {vulns}. Treat a string result as a failed check,
#   never as "no findings".
# - ERRORS are gRPC-status JSON: {"code": 3, "message": "invalid ecosystem"}
#   (code 3 = INVALID_ARGUMENT -> HTTP 400, code 5 = NOT_FOUND -> HTTP 404).
# - EXPERIMENTAL endpoints may change without notice: determine_version only
#   knows C/C++ libraries indexed for OSS-Fuzz and expects base64-encoded MD5
#   digests; list_import_findings is meant for operators of home databases;
#   map_ubuntu_binary_packages resolves at most 1,000 Ubuntu binary names.
# - NOT COVERED (other host): the bulk data exports in the public GCS bucket
#   gs://osv-vulnerabilities (per-ecosystem all.zip, modified_id.csv,
#   ecosystems.txt).

spec: "https://dadl.ai/spec/dadl-spec-v0.2.md"

credits:
  - "Dunkel Cloud GmbH -- maintainer"

source_name: "OSV.dev API"
source_url: "https://google.github.io/osv.dev/api/"
date: "2026-11-24"

backend:
  name: osv
  type: rest
  version: "1.0"
  base_url: "https://api.osv.dev"
  description: "OSV.dev open source vulnerability database API (public, no key): query known vulnerabilities by package and version, PURL or git commit (single and batched up to 1,000), fetch full OSV records by ID (GHSA, CVE, PYSEC, GO, RUSTSEC, Debian, Ubuntu, ...), run patch-level checks with fixed versions, plus experimental C/C++ version detection, Ubuntu binary-to-source mapping and import findings."

  # No authentication -- OSV.dev is fully public. ToolMesh requires an auth
  # block; the credential "none" is its placeholder for APIs without auth: it
  # is never looked up and no header or query parameter is injected.
  auth:
    type: apikey
    credential: none
    inject_into: header

  # Health: there is no credential to verify, so the check covers reachability
  # and the response format only. GET /v1/vulns/OSV-2020-111 is the cheapest
  # side-effect-free call (~1 KB, an OSS-Fuzz record unchanged since 2020); the
  # API has no dedicated status endpoint.
  health:
    method: GET
    path: /v1/vulns/OSV-2020-111
    expect_path: "$.id"
    timeout: 5s

  defaults:
    headers:
      Accept: application/json
    content_type: application/json
    # No default pagination block: OSV pages with a page_token in the JSON
    # body and a next_page_token in an object response, which ToolMesh's
    # automatic pagination cannot follow. Every tool sets pagination: none and
    # exposes the token explicitly.
    errors:
      format: json
      message_path: "$.message"
      code_path: "$.code"          # gRPC status code: 3 = invalid argument, 5 = not found
      retry_on: [429, 500, 502, 503, 504]
      terminal: [400, 401, 403, 404]
      retry_strategy:
        max_retries: 3
        backoff: exponential
        initial_delay: 1s
    response:
      result_path: "$"
      allow_jq_override: true

  types:
    OsvPackage:
      type: object
      description: "Package identity: name + ecosystem, plus the purl when the record has one."
      properties:
        ecosystem: { type: string }
        name: { type: string }
        purl: { type: string }
    OsvSeverity:
      type: object
      description: "Severity entry. score is a CVSS vector string (e.g. CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/...), not a numeric base score; type Ubuntu carries a priority word."
      properties:
        type: { type: string, enum: [CVSS_V2, CVSS_V3, CVSS_V4, Ubuntu] }
        score: { type: string }
        source: { type: string }
    OsvEvent:
      type: object
      description: "One range event; exactly one key is set. Versions for ECOSYSTEM/SEMVER ranges, commit hashes for GIT ranges."
      properties:
        introduced: { type: string }
        fixed: { type: string }
        last_affected: { type: string }
        limit: { type: string }
    OsvRange:
      type: object
      properties:
        type: { type: string, enum: [GIT, SEMVER, ECOSYSTEM] }
        repo: { type: string, description: "Repository URL (GIT ranges only)" }
        events: { type: array, items: OsvEvent }
    AffectedSummary:
      type: object
      properties:
        package: OsvPackage
        ranges: { type: array, items: OsvRange }
        fixed: { type: array, items: { type: string }, description: "fixed events of the ECOSYSTEM/SEMVER ranges (absent = no fix known)" }
        last_affected: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
        versions: { type: array, items: { type: string }, description: "Only present when the entry has no ranges" }
        severity: { type: array, items: OsvSeverity }
        severity_label: { type: string, description: "ecosystem_specific.severity / .urgency or database_specific.severity of this entry" }
    VulnSummary:
      type: object
      properties:
        id: { type: string }
        summary: { type: string, description: "Record summary, or the first 240 characters of details when the record has none" }
        aliases: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
        upstream: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
        published: { type: string }
        modified: { type: string }
        withdrawn: { type: string, description: "Set when the record was retracted -- ignore such records" }
        severity: { type: array, items: OsvSeverity }
        severity_label: { type: string, description: "database_specific.severity (e.g. GHSA LOW|MODERATE|HIGH|CRITICAL), else the Ubuntu priority from severity" }
        affected: { type: array, items: AffectedSummary }
      required: [id]
    VulnSummaryPage:
      type: object
      properties:
        vulns: { type: array, items: VulnSummary }
        next_page_token: { type: string, description: "Present when more results exist -- repeat the query with page_token" }
      required: [vulns]
    BatchVulnRef:
      type: object
      properties:
        id: { type: string }
        modified: { type: string }
    BatchResult:
      type: object
      properties:
        vulns: { type: array, items: BatchVulnRef }
        next_page_token: { type: string }
      required: [vulns]
    VersionMatch:
      type: object
      properties:
        score: { type: number, description: "0 < score <= 1, 1 = perfect match" }
        repo_info:
          type: object
          properties:
            type: { type: string }
            address: { type: string }
            tag: { type: string }
            version: { type: string }
            commit: { type: string }
        minimum_file_matches: { type: string }
        estimated_diff_files: { type: string }
    ImportFinding:
      type: object
      properties:
        bug_id: { type: string }
        source: { type: string }
        findings: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
        first_seen: { type: string }
        last_attempt: { type: string }
    SourcePackages:
      type: object
      properties:
        source_names: { type: array, items: { type: string } }

  tools:

    # =========================================================================
    # Vulnerability queries -- "which known vulnerabilities affect this package
    # version / this commit?" Single query (projected records), batched query
    # (ids only, up to 1,000 packages) and full record lookup by id.
    # =========================================================================

    query_vulnerabilities:
      method: POST
      path: /v1/query
      access: read
      retry_unsafe: true
      description: >
        List the known vulnerabilities affecting one package version or one git
        commit (POST /v1/query). Identify the package as {name, ecosystem} or as
        {purl}; ecosystem names are CASE-SENSITIVE ("PyPI", "npm", "Maven", "Go",
        "crates.io", "RubyGems", "NuGet", "Debian:12", "Ubuntu:24.04:LTS",
        "Alpine:v3.20"; a wrong spelling is HTTP 400 "invalid ecosystem"). Distro
        ecosystems need the release suffix -- plain "Debian" mixes all releases.
        Use the exact ecosystem version string (distros: full version incl. epoch
        and revision); omit version to get every vulnerability ever recorded for
        the package. Put the version in the purl OR in version, never both (400).
        Returns {vulns, next_page_token?}; each record is projected to id,
        summary (falls back to the first 240 chars of details), aliases,
        upstream, published, modified, withdrawn, severity (CVSS VECTOR strings,
        not numeric scores), severity_label (GHSA label such as HIGH, else the
        Ubuntu priority) and affected[]
        with package, ranges (introduced/fixed/last_affected events), fixed
        (fixed versions of the non-GIT ranges; absent = no fix known),
        last_affected and severity_label. affected[] lists EVERY package and
        distro release the record covers, not only the queried one -- match
        package.ecosystem and package.name. One range may hold several branches:
        pick the fix on the installed version's branch. The same vulnerability
        often appears under several ids (GHSA + PYSEC + CVE via aliases):
        deduplicate before counting. Ignore records with withdrawn set. Details,
        references, credits and the enumerated versions list are dropped: call
        get_vulnerability for the full record. An empty vulns list is NOT proof
        that the version is safe -- coverage depends on the upstream advisory
        feeds. PAGINATION: when next_page_token is present (more than ~1,000
        results, or the server's 20-second limit was hit, in which case a page
        can hold ZERO vulns plus a token), call again with the same query and
        page_token until no token comes back. SIZE LIMIT: ToolMesh reads at
        most 10 MiB of the raw response; above that (distro kernel packages
        such as Debian "linux": ~13 MB) the projection fails and the call
        returns a STRING instead of {vulns} -- treat that as a failed check,
        not as "no findings". Linux kernel version queries ({name: "Kernel",
        ecosystem: "Linux"}) take several 20-second rounds and returned no
        match for 6.6.1 although kernel CVEs exist: not usable for kernel
        checks.
      params:
        package:
          type: object
          in: body
          description: "Package to query: {name, ecosystem} (e.g. {name: 'jinja2', ecosystem: 'PyPI'}) or {purl} (e.g. {purl: 'pkg:npm/lodash@4.17.15'}). Optional for commit queries."
        version:
          type: string
          in: body
          description: "Exact version string in the ecosystem's format (e.g. '2.4.1', Debian '3.0.11-1~deb12u2'). Must not be combined with commit or a versioned purl."
        commit:
          type: string
          in: body
          description: "Full git commit hash to query GIT ranges for (mostly C/C++ projects). Must not be combined with version."
        page_token:
          type: string
          in: body
          description: "next_page_token from the previous response, sent together with the unchanged query."
      returns: VulnSummaryPage
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          def nn: with_entries(select(.value != null));
          def nonempty: if (. == null or length == 0) then null else . end;
          def sev_label: if type == "object" then (.severity // .urgency) else null end;
          {
            vulns: [(.vulns // [])[] | {
              id,
              summary: (.summary // ((.details // "") | .[0:240] | if . == "" then null else . end)),
              aliases: (.aliases | nonempty),
              upstream: (.upstream | nonempty),
              published,
              modified,
              withdrawn,
              severity: (.severity | nonempty),
              severity_label: (((.database_specific // null) | sev_label) // ([(.severity // [])[] | select(.type == "Ubuntu") | .score][0])),
              affected: [(.affected // [])[] | {
                package: (if .package == null then null else (.package | {ecosystem, name, purl} | nn) end),
                ranges: ([(.ranges // [])[] | {type, repo, events} | nn] | nonempty),
                fixed: ([(.ranges // [])[] | select(.type != "GIT") | (.events // [])[] | .fixed // empty] | nonempty),
                last_affected: ([(.ranges // [])[] | select(.type != "GIT") | (.events // [])[] | .last_affected // empty] | nonempty),
                versions: (if ((.ranges // []) | length) == 0 then (.versions | nonempty) else null end),
                severity: (.severity | nonempty),
                severity_label: (((.ecosystem_specific // null) | sev_label) // ((.database_specific // null) | sev_label))
              } | nn]
            } | nn],
            next_page_token
          } | nn
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    query_vulnerabilities_batch:
      method: POST
      path: /v1/querybatch
      access: read
      retry_unsafe: true
      description: >
        Check up to 1,000 package versions or commits in one request
        (POST /v1/querybatch). Each item of queries has the same shape as a
        query_vulnerabilities call: {package: {name, ecosystem} | {purl},
        version?} or {commit}, optionally page_token. Returns an array in the
        SAME ORDER as queries; each element is {vulns: [{id, modified}],
        next_page_token?} -- ids only, no summaries, severities or fixed
        versions. An unaffected item yields vulns: [] (NOT proof that it is
        safe; coverage depends on the upstream feeds). One invalid item (e.g.
        ecosystem "pypi" instead of "PyPI") fails the whole batch with HTTP 400
        "error in query at index N". Follow up with query_vulnerabilities for an
        affected package (projected records with fixed versions) or
        get_vulnerability for single ids -- or use the check_package_vulns
        composite, which does both. Records describing the same vulnerability
        (GHSA + PYSEC + CVE) are listed separately. Pagination is per item: an
        item carries next_page_token above ~1,000 vulns per item or ~3,000 in
        total; re-send only those items with page_token set.
      params:
        queries:
          type: array
          in: body
          required: true
          description: "Array of 1-1000 query objects, e.g. [{package: {name: 'jinja2', ecosystem: 'PyPI'}, version: '2.4.1'}, {package: {purl: 'pkg:npm/lodash@4.17.15'}}, {commit: '<40-hex sha>'}]."
      returns:
        type: array
        items: BatchResult
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.results // [])[] | {vulns: [(.vulns // [])[] | {id, modified}], next_page_token} | with_entries(select(.value != null))]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    get_vulnerability:
      method: GET
      path: /v1/vulns/{id}
      access: read
      description: >
        Get the complete OSV record for one vulnerability id (GET
        /v1/vulns/{id}): id, summary, details (Markdown), aliases, related,
        upstream, published, modified, withdrawn, severity (CVSS vector
        strings), affected[] (package, ranges with introduced/fixed/
        last_affected events, the enumerated versions list, ecosystem_specific,
        database_specific), references (advisories, fix commits, reports),
        credits, database_specific and schema_version. Ids are CASE-SENSITIVE
        ("GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45", not "ghsa-..."); an unknown id is HTTP 404
        "Vulnerability not found". Many CVE ids resolve directly (records
        converted from NVD, with GIT commit ranges); otherwise query by package
        and read the CVE from aliases/upstream. Records can be large (Log4Shell
        CVE-2021-44228 ~60 KB, Ubuntu records list every release): project the
        fields you need in code.
      params:
        id:
          type: string
          in: path
          required: true
          description: "OSV record id, case-sensitive, e.g. GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45, PYSEC-2019-217, GO-2024-2598, CVE-2021-44228, UBUNTU-CVE-2024-13176, OSV-2020-111."
      returns:
        type: object
        description: "Full OSV record (OSV schema), see https://ossf.github.io/osv-schema/"
        properties:
          id: { type: string }
          summary: { type: string }
          details: { type: string }
          aliases: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
          related: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
          upstream: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
          published: { type: string }
          modified: { type: string }
          withdrawn: { type: string }
          severity: { type: array, items: OsvSeverity }
          affected: { type: array, items: { type: object } }
          references: { type: array, items: { type: object } }
          credits: { type: array, items: { type: object } }
          database_specific: { type: object }
          schema_version: { type: string }
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    # =========================================================================
    # Experimental endpoints (/v1experimental/) -- documented by OSV.dev as
    # experimental; shape and availability may change without notice.
    # =========================================================================

    determine_version:
      method: POST
      path: /v1experimental/determineversion
      access: read
      retry_unsafe: true
      description: >
        Identify the upstream library and version of a C/C++ source tree from
        file hashes, e.g. for vendored code without a package manager
        (EXPERIMENTAL endpoint POST /v1experimental/determineversion, may
        change without notice). Only libraries indexed
        for OSS-Fuzz are known. Hash every .c, .cc, .h, .hh, .cpp and .hpp file
        of the library: file_hashes = [{file_path: 'src/zlib.h' (relative to the
        library root), hash: '<base64 of the raw 16-byte MD5 digest, NOT hex>'}].
        Returns matches sorted by score (1.0 = perfect), each with repo_info
        {type, address, tag, version}, minimum_file_matches and
        estimated_diff_files (int64 values as strings). An empty array means no
        match. Then query the likely versions with query_vulnerabilities_batch
        (ecosystem "GIT", name = repo_info.address, version = repo_info.tag).
      params:
        name:
          type: string
          in: body
          description: "Optional library name to narrow the search, e.g. 'protobuf'."
        file_hashes:
          type: array
          in: body
          required: true
          description: "Array of {file_path, hash} -- hash is the base64-encoded raw MD5 digest of the file."
      returns:
        type: array
        items: VersionMatch
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          .matches // []
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    map_ubuntu_binary_packages:
      method: POST
      path: /v1experimental/ubuntu/binary-to-source
      access: read
      retry_unsafe: true
      description: >
        Map Ubuntu BINARY package names (as listed by dpkg/apt, e.g.
        libssl3t64, libcurl4t64) to the SOURCE package names that OSV's Ubuntu
        records are indexed by (EXPERIMENTAL endpoint POST
        /v1experimental/ubuntu/binary-to-source). Run this before querying
        Ubuntu packages by name: query_vulnerabilities with ecosystem
        "Ubuntu:24.04:LTS" expects "openssl", not "libssl3t64". Up to 1,000
        names per call. Returns an array in the SAME ORDER as binary_names, each
        {source_names: [...]}; an empty source_names means the binary is unknown
        in that release (binary names change between releases, e.g. libcurl4 on
        22.04 vs libcurl4t64 on 24.04). The ecosystem must carry a release
        ("Ubuntu:24.04", "Ubuntu:24.04:LTS", "Ubuntu:Pro:24.04:LTS"); other
        distros are rejected with HTTP 400.
      params:
        ecosystem:
          type: string
          in: body
          required: true
          description: "Ubuntu release ecosystem, e.g. 'Ubuntu:24.04:LTS', 'Ubuntu:22.04:LTS', 'Ubuntu:Pro:20.04:LTS'."
        binary_names:
          type: array
          in: body
          required: true
          description: "Non-empty array of up to 1000 Ubuntu binary package names."
      returns:
        type: array
        items: SourcePackages
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.results // [])[] | {source_names: (.source_names // [])}]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    list_import_findings:
      method: GET
      path: /v1experimental/importfindings/{source}
      access: read
      description: >
        List the records of one OSV.dev data source that failed OSV.dev's
        import-time quality checks and were therefore NOT imported
        (EXPERIMENTAL endpoint GET /v1experimental/importfindings/{source}).
        Meant for operators of
        home databases that publish OSV records; also useful to explain why a
        known advisory is missing from query results. source is the source name
        from OSV.dev's source.yaml (case-sensitive, lowercase, e.g. 'ghsa').
        Returns an array of {bug_id, source, findings: [IMPORT_FINDING_TYPE_...],
        first_seen, last_attempt}. An empty array means no findings -- an
        unknown source name ALSO returns an empty array, not an error.
      params:
        source:
          type: string
          in: path
          required: true
          description: "Data source name as in OSV.dev's source.yaml (name field), e.g. 'ghsa'."
      returns:
        type: array
        items: ImportFinding
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          .invalid_records // []
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

  # ===========================================================================
  # Composites
  # ===========================================================================
  composites:

    check_package_vulns:
      access: read
      description: >
        Patch-level check for a list of package versions (or commits): one
        query_vulnerabilities_batch call, then query_vulnerabilities for every
        affected item, merged into findings per input item. Input queries: the
        same objects as for query_vulnerabilities_batch (1-1000). Returns
        {checked, vulnerable, detail_budget_exhausted, results: [{index, query,
        vulnerable, finding_count, findings: [{ids, aliases, upstream, summary,
        severity, severity_label, fixed, last_affected, ranges}],
        package_match, incomplete, detailed, record_ids, withdrawn_ids,
        error}]} in input order. A finding is one vulnerability: records linked
        via aliases (GHSA + PYSEC + CVE) are merged; distro advisories that
        bundle several CVEs (DSA, DLA, USN) stay separate. fixed and ranges are
        taken from the affected entries matching the queried package (ecosystem
        incl. release prefix + normalized name, or purl without version);
        package_match false means no entry matched and all entries were used.
        fixed lists every fixed version of the matched ranges (several branches
        possible -- pick the one on the installed version's branch; empty = no
        fix known). At most 49 affected items get details per run (sandbox limit
        of 50 calls); the rest come back with detailed: false and record_ids
        only -- call again with those items. An item whose detail query failed
        or exceeded ToolMesh's 10 MiB response limit (distro kernel packages)
        keeps vulnerable: true with detailed: false, record_ids and error.
        incomplete: true means OSV paginated (>1,000 results or its 20-second
        limit): use query_vulnerabilities with page_token for that item.
        vulnerable: false is NOT proof of "clean" -- coverage depends on the
        upstream feeds.
      params:
        queries:
          type: array
          required: true
          description: "Array of 1-1000 query objects: {package: {name, ecosystem} | {purl}, version?} or {commit}."
      timeout: 120s
      depends_on: [query_vulnerabilities_batch, query_vulnerabilities]
      code: |
        const queries = params.queries;
        if (!Array.isArray(queries) || queries.length === 0) {
          throw new Error("queries must be a non-empty array of OSV query objects");
        }
        if (queries.length > 1000) {
          throw new Error("at most 1000 queries per call (OSV querybatch limit)");
        }
        // 50 api.* calls per composite run; one is the batch call.
        let budget = 49;

        const normName = (s) => String(s || "").toLowerCase().replace(/[-_.]+/g, "-");
        const purlBase = (p) => {
          let s = String(p || "");
          try { s = decodeURIComponent(s); } catch (e) { /* keep the raw purl */ }
          s = s.split("#")[0].split("?")[0];
          const at = s.lastIndexOf("@");
          if (at > s.lastIndexOf("/")) s = s.slice(0, at);
          return s.toLowerCase();
        };
        const matchesQuery = (q, aff) => {
          const ap = aff.package || {};
          const qp = q.package || {};
          if (qp.purl) return !!ap.purl && purlBase(ap.purl) === purlBase(qp.purl);
          if (qp.name && qp.ecosystem) {
            const ae = String(ap.ecosystem || "");
            const sameEco = ae === qp.ecosystem || ae.indexOf(qp.ecosystem + ":") === 0;
            return sameEco && normName(ap.name) === normName(qp.name);
          }
          return false;
        };
        const uniq = (arr) => Array.from(new Set(arr));
        const uniqBy = (arr, keyFn) => {
          const seen = {};
          return arr.filter((x) => {
            const k = keyFn(x);
            if (seen[k]) return false;
            seen[k] = true;
            return true;
          });
        };

        const batch = await api.query_vulnerabilities_batch({ queries: queries });
        if (!Array.isArray(batch)) {
          // A string here means the raw batch response was cut off at ToolMesh's
          // 10 MiB limit and the projection could not run.
          throw new Error("querybatch response could not be parsed (over 10 MiB?) -- split the queries into smaller batches");
        }
        const results = [];

        for (let i = 0; i < queries.length; i++) {
          const q = queries[i] || {};
          const r = (batch && batch[i]) || {};
          const refs = r.vulns || [];
          const entry = { index: i, query: q, vulnerable: refs.length > 0 };
          if (r.next_page_token) entry.incomplete = true;
          if (refs.length === 0) {
            entry.finding_count = 0;
            results.push(entry);
            continue;
          }
          if (budget <= 0) {
            entry.detailed = false;
            entry.record_ids = refs.map((v) => v.id);
            results.push(entry);
            continue;
          }
          budget--;

          const args = {};
          if (q.package) args.package = q.package;
          if (q.version) args.version = q.version;
          if (q.commit) args.commit = q.commit;
          let page;
          try {
            page = await api.query_vulnerabilities(args);
          } catch (e) {
            entry.detailed = false;
            entry.record_ids = refs.map((v) => v.id);
            entry.error = String((e && e.message) || e);
            results.push(entry);
            continue;
          }
          if (!page || typeof page !== "object" || !Array.isArray(page.vulns)) {
            // Never report "not affected" for an unparseable detail response:
            // the raw body exceeded ToolMesh's 10 MiB limit (distro kernel
            // packages) or the projection failed.
            entry.detailed = false;
            entry.record_ids = refs.map((v) => v.id);
            entry.error = "detail response could not be parsed (raw response over ToolMesh's 10 MiB limit?)";
            results.push(entry);
            continue;
          }
          if (page.next_page_token) entry.incomplete = true;

          const records = page.vulns;
          const live = records.filter((v) => !v.withdrawn);
          const withdrawnIds = records.filter((v) => v.withdrawn).map((v) => v.id);
          if (withdrawnIds.length > 0) entry.withdrawn_ids = withdrawnIds;

          // Merge records that describe the same vulnerability (alias-linked).
          let groups = [];
          for (const rec of live) {
            const keys = new Set([rec.id].concat(rec.aliases || []));
            const merged = { keys: keys, recs: [rec] };
            const rest = [];
            for (const g of groups) {
              let overlaps = false;
              g.keys.forEach((k) => { if (keys.has(k)) overlaps = true; });
              if (overlaps) {
                g.keys.forEach((k) => merged.keys.add(k));
                merged.recs = merged.recs.concat(g.recs);
              } else {
                rest.push(g);
              }
            }
            rest.push(merged);
            groups = rest;
          }

          let packageMatched = true;
          entry.findings = groups.map((g) => {
            const ids = g.recs.map((v) => v.id).sort();
            const allAff = [];
            g.recs.forEach((v) => (v.affected || []).forEach((a) => allAff.push(a)));
            let aff = allAff.filter((a) => matchesQuery(q, a));
            if (aff.length === 0) {
              aff = allAff;
              packageMatched = false;
            }
            let severity = [];
            g.recs.forEach((v) => { severity = severity.concat(v.severity || []); });
            aff.forEach((a) => { severity = severity.concat(a.severity || []); });
            severity = uniqBy(severity, (s) => s.type + "|" + s.score);
            let label = null;
            g.recs.forEach((v) => { if (!label && v.severity_label) label = v.severity_label; });
            aff.forEach((a) => { if (!label && a.severity_label) label = a.severity_label; });
            let fixed = [];
            let lastAffected = [];
            let ranges = [];
            aff.forEach((a) => {
              fixed = fixed.concat(a.fixed || []);
              lastAffected = lastAffected.concat(a.last_affected || []);
              ranges = ranges.concat(a.ranges || []);
            });
            let upstream = [];
            g.recs.forEach((v) => { upstream = upstream.concat(v.upstream || []); });
            const summaryRec = g.recs.find((v) => v.summary);
            const finding = {
              ids: ids,
              aliases: uniq(Array.from(g.keys).filter((k) => ids.indexOf(k) < 0)).sort(),
              summary: summaryRec ? summaryRec.summary : null,
              severity: severity,
              fixed: uniq(fixed)
            };
            if (label) finding.severity_label = label;
            if (upstream.length > 0) finding.upstream = uniq(upstream).sort();
            if (lastAffected.length > 0) finding.last_affected = uniq(lastAffected);
            finding.ranges = uniqBy(ranges, (x) => JSON.stringify(x));
            return finding;
          });
          entry.vulnerable = entry.findings.length > 0;
          entry.finding_count = entry.findings.length;
          if (entry.findings.length > 0) entry.package_match = packageMatched;
          results.push(entry);
        }

        return {
          checked: queries.length,
          vulnerable: results.filter((x) => x.vulnerable).length,
          detail_budget_exhausted: results.some((x) => x.detailed === false && !x.error),
          results: results
        };

  coverage:
    endpoints: 6
    total_endpoints: 6
    percentage: 100
    focus: "OSV.dev vulnerability API: vulnerabilities (package versions, PURLs, git commits, IDs, batches of 1,000), packages (patch-level checks with fixed versions and severity), versions (C/C++ source fingerprinting), Ubuntu (binary-to-source mapping), import findings (record quality)."
    missing: "Every documented REST endpoint is covered (three of them are experimental). Not covered because they live on another host: the bulk data exports in the public GCS bucket gs://osv-vulnerabilities (per-ecosystem all.zip archives, modified_id.csv, ecosystems.txt). The osv.dev web UI and the osv-scanner CLI are separate products."
    last_reviewed: "2026-11-24"

  setup:
    credential_steps:
      - "No credentials are required -- the OSV.dev API is public and anonymous."
      - "No signup, API key, token or OAuth client is needed for any endpoint."
      - "Keep credential: none in the DADL; ToolMesh then sends every request without authentication."
    env_var: ""
    backends_yaml: |
      - name: osv
        transport: rest
        dadl: osv.dadl
        url: "https://api.osv.dev"
        options:
          timeout: "60s"
    required_scopes: []
    optional_scopes: []
    docs_url: "https://google.github.io/osv.dev/api/"
    notes: >
      Public read API operated by Google's open source security team (osv.dev,
      source code Apache-2.0). No authentication and no documented rate limit.
      Base URL is https://api.osv.dev; the file uses full paths because the
      stable endpoints live under /v1/ and the experimental ones under
      /v1experimental/. OpenAPI (Swagger 2.0) spec:
      https://osv.dev/docs/osv_service_v1.swagger.json . OSV schema reference:
      https://ossf.github.io/osv-schema/ . Valid ecosystem names:
      https://osv-vulnerabilities.storage.googleapis.com/ecosystems.txt .
      The health check fetches one small fixed record (OSV-2020-111): with no
      credential involved it verifies reachability and the response format only.
      OSV limits responses to 32 MiB over HTTP/1.1 and pages very broad queries
      after 20 seconds of server time, so the backends.yaml entry above raises
      the ToolMesh request timeout to 60 s (default 30 s). ToolMesh reads at
      most 10 MiB per response: distro kernel packages (Debian/Ubuntu "linux",
      ~13 MB) exceed that and cannot be checked through this backend.

  examples:
    - name: "Patch-level check for several packages"
      description: "Check installed package versions and report findings with severity and fixed versions"
      code: |
        const report = await api.check_package_vulns({
          queries: [
            { package: { name: "jinja2", ecosystem: "PyPI" }, version: "2.10" },
            { package: { purl: "pkg:npm/lodash@4.17.15" } },
            { package: { name: "openssl", ecosystem: "Debian:12" }, version: "3.0.11-1~deb12u2" }
          ]
        });
        return report.results.map((r) => ({
          query: r.query,
          findings: (r.findings || []).map((f) => ({ ids: f.ids, label: f.severity_label, fixed: f.fixed }))
        }));
    - name: "Follow pagination with page_token"
      description: "Collect all results of a broad query that OSV returns in pages (or after its 20-second limit)"
      code: |
        const query = { package: { name: "openssl", ecosystem: "Debian:12" } };
        let all = [];
        let token;
        for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
          const page = await api.query_vulnerabilities(token ? Object.assign({}, query, { page_token: token }) : query);
          if (typeof page === "string") throw new Error("response over the 10 MiB limit -- narrow the query");
          all = all.concat(page.vulns);
          token = page.next_page_token;
          if (!token) break;
        }
        return { count: all.length, complete: !token, ids: all.map((v) => v.id) };
    - name: "Full record for one advisory"
      description: "Fetch a vulnerability by id and keep the fields needed for a report"
      code: |
        const v = await api.get_vulnerability({ id: "GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45" });
        return {
          id: v.id,
          aliases: v.aliases,
          summary: v.summary,
          severity: v.severity,
          fix_refs: (v.references || []).filter((r) => r.type === "FIX").map((r) => r.url)
        };
